{
  "title": "Discard stale recipe-list responses on both clients",
  "description": "CookbookPage starts a new listRecipes request whenever the \
URL query or sort changes, but every response updates recipes, totals, \
offsets, and loading state regardless of whether a newer request has started. \
A slow response for an old search can therefore replace the results for the \
current URL. Append requests have the same risk when a filter change races an \
in-flight next-page request. The page already uses a monotonically increasing \
request ID for bulk-selection requests. iOS checks `activeQuery` before \
applying an initial response, but `RecipeListViewModel.loadMore()` appends a \
response without rechecking the query, so an old page can be mixed into the \
new list there as well.\n\
\n\
Scope:\n\
- Give cookbook list requests an identity or abort signal and only apply data, \
error, pagination, and loading-state updates from the current request.\n\
- Ensure a filter/sort change invalidates both initial and append requests.\n\
- Extract the request-state logic if needed to make it directly unit-testable, \
without redesigning the page.\n\
- Add web unit tests with controllable deferred responses proving that older \
success and failure responses cannot overwrite newer state.\n\
- Apply the same request identity rule to iOS pagination and add mirrored \
RecipeListViewModel tests for a filter or sort change during `loadMore()`. \
Keep the existing initial-response guard.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 1,
  "type": "task",
